  <h2>At the Station: A Walkthrough</h2>
  <h3>Before You Go</h3>
  <p>Arrive 45-60 minutes before departure for high-speed trains (G/D trains), 30 minutes for regular trains. Chinese stations are large — the walk from the entrance to your platform can take 10+ minutes. Have your passport ready at all times — you'll show it at security, ticket collection, and boarding.</p>
  <h3>Security Check</h3>
  <p>All passengers go through security — bags through X-ray, walk through a metal detector. Similar to airport security but faster. <strong>Note:</strong> aerosol cans (deodorant, sunscreen) are often confiscated — pack these in checked luggage or buy them at your destination. Power banks must be under 20,000 mAh and clearly labeled with capacity.</p>
  <h3>Finding Your Gate</h3>
  <p>Look for the giant departure board — it cycles between Chinese and English. Find your train number (e.g., G2902). The board shows: train number → destination → departure time → gate number → status. Screenshot the board or write down your gate number. Gates open about 15 minutes before departure and close 3-5 minutes before.</p>
  <h3>Boarding</h3>
  <p>Line up at your gate. Scan your ticket (or passport at newer stations) at the turnstile. Follow the crowd down to the platform. Check the platform signs — they indicate which car number stops where. High-speed trains are long (16 cars), and walking between cars is possible but awkward with luggage. Board through the door closest to your assigned car.</p>
  <h3>On the Train</h3>
  <p>Find your seat. Luggage goes on the overhead rack (smaller bags) or in the luggage area at the end of each car. There's usually a hot water dispenser near the carriage entrance — bring instant noodles or tea bags. The dining car serves hot meals (30-50 RMB) and snacks, but instant noodles are the traditional train food in China. Bathrooms are at the end of each car — bring your own tissue paper.</p>

  <h2>Seat Selection Tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Window seats (A/F):</strong> Best for views and sleeping. The A seat is always by the window. On G-trains, the scenery between Guangzhou and Guilin includes mountains and rivers — worth the window seat.</li>
    <li><strong>Aisle seats (C/D):</strong> More legroom to stretch, easier bathroom access on long trips.</li>
    <li><strong>Forward-facing:</strong> Chinese high-speed seats can be rotated. If your seat faces backward and it bothers you, ask a conductor to rotate it — they'll do it at the next stop.</li>
  </ul>

